From: Lorenzo Giannoccaro <giannoccarol@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 19:50:58 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: HFP: let devices turn off wideband speech

get_wbs_supported() always returned true, so a headset that supports
mSBC always got a wideband SCO link. Read bluetooth.hfp.wbs.supported
(default true) so that devices whose controller cannot carry mSBC on
the PCM path can stay on CVSD.

Change-Id: I67ee071c065b190c7710872b3c00536f3f12c164
---
 system/stack/btm/btm_sco_hfp_hal.cc | 5 +++--
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/system/stack/btm/btm_sco_hfp_hal.cc b/system/stack/btm/btm_sco_hfp_hal.cc
index c2c7239..c8c625c 100644
--- a/system/stack/btm/btm_sco_hfp_hal.cc
+++ b/system/stack/btm/btm_sco_hfp_hal.cc
@@ -29,8 +29,9 @@ void init() {
 // This is not used in Android.
 bool is_coding_format_supported(esco_coding_format_t /* coding_format */) { return true; }
 
-// Android statically compiles WBS support.
-bool get_wbs_supported() { return true; }
+// Android statically compiles WBS support. Devices whose controller cannot
+// carry mSBC on the PCM path can turn it off and keep calls on CVSD.
+bool get_wbs_supported() { return osi_property_get_bool("bluetooth.hfp.wbs.supported", true); }
 
 // Software path implies support of SWB.
 bool get_swb_supported() {
-- 
